Subject: Add `--use-distinct-char8-t`, recognising `char8_t` by its spelling

C++20's `char8_t` is the one builtin type bindgen cannot see. libclang has no
`CXType_Char8` - the kinds run `CXType_UChar = 5`, `CXType_Char16 = 6`,
`CXType_Char32 = 7`, and nothing was added for it - so `build_builtin_ty`
returns `None`, `Type::from_clang_ty` falls through to the unexposed arm, and a
`char8_t` parameter comes out as an opaque one-byte blob. That is the same
outcome bindgen produces for a type it genuinely does not understand, which is
misleading: it understands this one perfectly, it just has no enumerator to
match on.

It does not need one. clang spells the type `char8_t`, and bindgen already
wraps `clang_getTypeSpelling` as `Type::spelling` and already stores the
spelling on every builtin it constructs. `Type::is_char8_t` uses that, checked
against libclang 17 and 21 on the way in. Qualifiers are part of a spelling, so
the comparison is against the last word, and the type's layout is checked as
well: before C++20 `char8_t` is an ordinary identifier, so a template type
parameter can be named that and arrives spelled and kinded identically, and
`spelling` strips a `T::` qualification too. clang gives the real type a
one-byte layout and answers `CXTypeLayoutError_Dependent` for either of those.

`--use-distinct-char8-t` then emits a fake type called `bindgen_cchar8_t`, in
the same shape as `--use-distinct-char16-t`'s `bindgen_cchar16_t`, for a
downstream post-processor to replace. `IntKind::Char8` is unsigned and one byte
wide by [basic.fundamental]/7, so `is_signed` and `known_size` answer for it
directly.

Off by default, so no existing output changes - including the opaque blob,
which is what a caller who does not ask still gets.

--- a/clang.rs
+++ b/clang.rs
@@ -1430,6 +1430,31 @@
         unsafe { clang_isConstQualifiedType(self.x) != 0 }
     }
 
+    /// Is this C++20's `char8_t`?
+    ///
+    /// There is no `CXType_Char8` to compare against: libclang's kinds go
+    /// `CXType_UChar`, `CXType_Char16`, `CXType_Char32`, with no slot for the
+    /// type C++20 added, so `char8_t` arrives as `CXType_Unexposed` and only
+    /// its spelling says what it is. Qualifiers are part of that spelling -
+    /// `const char8_t` - so the last word is what is compared.
+    ///
+    /// The spelling alone would not be enough. Before C++20 `char8_t` is an
+    /// ordinary identifier, so a template type parameter can be named that,
+    /// and such a parameter is unexposed and spelled `char8_t` as well;
+    /// `spelling` drops a qualification whose every segment is an identifier,
+    /// so a dependent `T::char8_t` arrives spelled the same way too. What
+    /// separates them is the layout: clang answers one byte for the real type
+    /// and `CXTypeLayoutError_Dependent` for either of those.
+    pub(crate) fn is_char8_t(&self, ctx: &BindgenContext) -> bool {
+        if self.kind() != CXType_Unexposed {
+            return false;
+        }
+        if !matches!(self.spelling().rsplit(' ').next(), Some("char8_t")) {
+            return false;
+        }
+        matches!(self.fallible_size(ctx), Ok(1))
+    }
+
     #[inline]
     fn is_non_deductible_auto_type(&self) -> bool {
         debug_assert_eq!(self.kind(), CXType_Auto);
